  "body": [
    "Register for Fall Programming! Session runs now - Nov 8.",
    "PLAY PLAY submenu Programs Private Lessons Adult Camps Summer Camps Book a Court Free30 Dave Bailey Programs",
    "EVENTS EVENTS submenu Calendar 2026 BNP Paribas World Team Cup",
    "ABOUT ABOUT submenu Meet Our Team Contact Us Sponsors & Partners USTA National Campus Scholarship",
    "WATCH WATCH submenu Watch Live Record Your Match",
    "VISIT VISIT submenu Hotel Booking Sustainability Health & Wellness Shop",
    "Home >",
    "News >",
    "Ten More Titles Earned at 101st ATA National Championships",
    "Another weather delay prolonged day six of the 101st American Tennis Association (ATA) National Championships, hosted by the USTA National Campus. The slate of matches was highlighted by seven singles, one doubles and two mixed doubles crown being claimed over the course of the day.",
    "Looking ahead to Friday, the remaining junior singles along with all junior doubles divisions are set to conclude. The featured matches will include the boys’ and girls’ 18s singles finals, which will be part of a live broadcast starting at 11:30 a.m. Top seed Kent Hunter (Los Angeles) and No. 3 seed Raymond Richardson (Chicago) will battle for the boys’ 18s title while No. 1 Azaria Hayes (Pinole, Calif.) will face off with second seed Hana Moss (Culver City, Calif.) in the girls’ 18s final.",
    "Below are the results from the 10 championship matches that were played on Thursday.",
    "Girls’ 10s Singles: Aleynah Thomas (Hephzibah, Ga.) def. No. 1 Athina Bezianis (Kensington, Md.) 6-3, 6-2",
    "Men’s 45s Singles: No. 2 Ralph Simpson (Newark, Del.) def. No. 1 Larry Scheper (Beaufort, S.C.) 6-7(3) 7-5, 6-2",
    "Men’s 55s Singles: No. 3 Lonnie White (Conway, S.C.) def. No. 1 Daryl McClellan (Newburgh, Ind.) 6-3, 3-6, 7-5",
    "Men’s 60s Singles: No. 1 Michael Harden (Augusta, Ga.) def. No. 4 Riley Washington (Saint Paul, Minn.), Walkover",
    "Men’s 75s Singles: No. 1 Phillip Lucas (Upper Marlboro, Md.) def. Denton Johnson (Seabrook, S.C.) 6-2, 6-0",
    "Men’s 80s Singles: Arthar Bush (Columbia, S.C.) def. David Walker (Washington, D.C.) 6-2, 5-7, 7-5",
    "Women’s 50s Singles: No. 3 Lisa Robinson (Duluth, Ga.) def. No. 1 Shelena Sanderson (Owings Mills, Md.) 6-2, 6-0",
    "Men’s 50s Doubles: Kenneth Myers (Shreveport, La.)/Larry Scheper (Beaufort, S.C.) def. Carlos Baez (Manati, P.R.)/Larry Mulligan-Gibbs (Elkins Park, Pa.) 6-2, 6-3",
    "Mixed 40s: No. 1 Chuck Jones (Glenside, Pa.)/Shana Smith (Sicklerville, N.J.) def. Frank Scott (Philadelphia)/Kimberly Scott (Atlanta), Walkover",
    "Mixed 75s: Ralph Harris (Upper Marlboro, Md.)/Roz King (San Diego) def. Lavenia Ferguson (Novelty, Ohio)/Raymond Games (Pell City, Ala.) 6-2, 6-2",
    "Play continues Friday morning at 8:00 a.m. For more information on the 2018 ATA National Championships, click here. To learn more about the history of the ATA, click here.",
    "The ATA was born on November 30, 1916, when representatives from over a dozen black tennis clubs met in Washington, D.C., with the goal of promoting black tennis in America. The organization held the first ATA National Championships at Baltimore’s Druid Hill Park in August of the following year; the tournament featured three total draws – men’s singles, women’s singles, and men’s doubles.",
    "The ATA, which is the oldest African-American sports organization in the United States, has produced a number of the world’s top players and coaches. Most notably among these are Althea Gibson and Arthur Ashe, the first African-Americans to be ranked No. 1 and to win grand slam titles. Others to grow up training at ATA-affiliated clubs and participating in ATA events include current USTA Chairman of the Board and President Katrina Adams, Zina Garrison, Leslie Allen, Lori McNeil, Chanda Rubin and MaliVai Washington.",
    "Monday - Friday 7:00 AM - 9:00 PM",
    "Saturday 7:00 AM - 5:00 PM",
    "Sunday 7:00 AM - 5:00 PM",
